WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:02.435
Thank you for joining us today!

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:04.935
My name is Bradley&nbsp;Knapp, and I'm one of the product managers

00:00:04.935 --> 00:00:06.256
here at IBM Cloud

00:00:06.256 --> 00:00:08.300
and we've come to answer the question:

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.600
what is Site Reliability Engineering, or SRE?

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.280
And SRE is really the name for a new discipline&nbsp;
that's actually an old discipline. &nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.366
It's a new name, it's only been around 15, 18 years,

00:00:20.366 --> 00:00:23.300
but&nbsp;the job itself has been around for a very long time.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.099
It's just evolved over time,

00:00:25.099 --> 00:00:29.231
and now we've&nbsp;given a formal name to the discipline and the job.

00:00:29.231 --> 00:00:35.566
And so, the question is, what is SRE,&nbsp;
what is site reliability engineering?

00:00:35.566 --> 00:00:38.436
And so, the way that I like to describe it

00:00:38.436 --> 00:00:45.284
is that it's really the collision of the&nbsp;
traditional IT role and DevOps, right?

00:00:45.284 --> 00:00:48.797
So, back in the day, in the traditional IT&nbsp;
role, you would think about

00:00:48.797 --> 00:00:52.939
lots of people sitting in an operations center staring at very&nbsp;
large screens,

00:00:52.939 --> 00:00:57.734
kind of arranged in a semicircle, like a mission center, or a&nbsp;
watch center in the military.

00:00:57.734 --> 00:01:01.812
Well, that world doesn't so much exist anymore,

00:01:01.812 --> 00:01:04.287
and in the new world, in the DevOps cycle

00:01:04.287 --> 00:01:07.849
that everyone should be embracing for their software&nbsp;releases,

00:01:07.849 --> 00:01:10.000
you still have to have reliability.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.373
Your developers are still going to engineer&nbsp;
the software to be reliable,

00:01:14.373 --> 00:01:19.703
but when it comes to actually operating it actually delivering&nbsp;
the service that goes out to the end customer,

00:01:19.703 --> 00:01:24.761
that's really kind of outside of the&nbsp;responsibility of those software developers.

00:01:24.761 --> 00:01:26.768
That's where SRE comes in.

00:01:26.768 --> 00:01:30.928
An SRE is&nbsp;what I like to call a 50/50 role, right?

00:01:30.928 --> 00:01:34.159
SREs should spend about 50% of their time

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:36.953
focusing on solving customer issues.

00:01:36.953 --> 00:01:39.840
That can be&nbsp;escalations, could be responding to incidents,

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:45.040
dealing with an upset customer who&nbsp;
needs help on a tactical problem.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:48.160
That's going to be 50, and then the other 50%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:53.440
is maybe the most important part, and&nbsp;
that's every SRE should be actively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.880
trying to automate themselves out of a job.&nbsp;
They want to automate all of the things.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:03.600
The buzzword for this is reducing toil&nbsp;
, right? Reducing all of the manual work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:08.800
necessary to keep any kind of&nbsp;
software environment up and running.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.760
This includes the hardware itself,&nbsp;
it includes all of the middleware,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:17.200
it includes the software - all of the related&nbsp;
services you have to keep these things live.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:22.240
And so, the question then becomes: all right,&nbsp;
well, we're going to automate these things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:26.400
isn't that putting my job at risk&nbsp;
if we get rid of these manual tasks?

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:32.400
And the answer is: in reality, no it's not.&nbsp;
It's never going to put your job at risk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.080
because every time you automate something,&nbsp;
you gain some additional insight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:39.840
into the system. Every time you automate&nbsp;
something, you learn something new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.160
and you identify additional tasks that&nbsp;
you'll be able to automate in the future.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:50.720
And so, automation is core. It's approaching&nbsp;
operations with a development mindset,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.480
because you want to programmatically&nbsp;
solve problems so that you don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.800
to go in and make the same manual&nbsp;
fix time after time after time.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.800
This is key to the SRE role, and&nbsp;
it's key to your success in it.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:07.920
And so that other 50% of the time I talked&nbsp;
about that before right, that's going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.640
escalations. It's going to be on-call work or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:14.400
in some cases, for a large enough&nbsp;
organization, SRE might be 24-7.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:20.160
It's going to include customer facing work, right?&nbsp;
You are going to have to interact with customers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:27.440
and it's going to include being the&nbsp;
source of knowledge for your group.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.480
Because SRE crosses all boundaries: it&nbsp;
knows about hardware, it knows about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.360
software, it knows about monitoring, it knows&nbsp;
about logging, it knows about automation.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:43.280
And so, they understand all of the different&nbsp;
components. They have the institutional knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.960
of how to keep the product up&nbsp;
and running as a product manager.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:51.040
I like to make the joke that when I want&nbsp;
to know how software's designed to run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:55.360
I go, and I ask the developers who wrote it.&nbsp;
When I want to know how it actually runs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:00.400
I go, and I ask SRE because they're the ones who&nbsp;
get to deal with the implementation every day.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:06.640
And so, bridging the gap between what&nbsp;
actually happens and what we want to happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:12.640
that's so important to the SRE job because&nbsp;
they have day-to-day hands-on interaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:15.440
with how people actually use the product.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:21.440
So, SRE is constantly feeding data back&nbsp;
into development so that development can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:26.800
make the software better, at the same time that&nbsp;
they're automating in all of the resiliency.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:30.480
SRE understands that failure will happen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:34.480
Failure is just the nature of business.&nbsp;
You cannot design a perfect system.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:39.360
And so, what SRE excels at is&nbsp;
programmatically identifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:44.480
potential failures and solving them ahead&nbsp;
of time, and it's also good at identifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:49.840
how are we going to solve&nbsp;
immediate tactical problems.

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:55.600
And so, I talked a minute ago about monitoring,&nbsp;
right? That traditional IT room with all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:01.600
screens. Well, monitoring and logging&nbsp;
are just key to the SRT role, SRE role.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:08.720
So SREs, as they monitor, they're keeping track&nbsp;
of what's happening in real time. Logging is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:12.400
archive of everything that's happened, so&nbsp;
that you can go back and examine it later.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:17.360
So, your monitoring is going to give&nbsp;
you the ability to anticipate failures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:20.560
and see them coming so that&nbsp;
you can proactively solve them.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:25.680
Logging is when you get an unanticipated&nbsp;
failure. It allows you to go back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:32.480
see what happened. You can do a an RCA, a Root&nbsp;
Cause Analysis , on it and figure out how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:38.560
solve it, not just for now, but for the future.&nbsp;
That gets back into the automation again, right?

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:41.360
If you know what happened,&nbsp;
and you know why it happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:45.360
you can then adjust that monitoring that we&nbsp;
were talking about, so that the monitoring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:52.000
itself will catch this edge case and you&nbsp;
don't encounter that failure ever again.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:59.040
So, SRE is just core to a successful business,&nbsp;
and most companies will find they have a role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:03.520
pretty similar to SRE today in the world&nbsp;
of software in the world of technology it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:07.200
something that we already have, even&nbsp;
though we may not be calling it SRE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:12.160
but if you're talking to a startup, a very&nbsp;
young company, they're going to say, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:16.800
you know we don't have the budget to go out&nbsp;
and develop an SRE organization to start with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:21.760
right? We only have 25 employees, we&nbsp;
only have 30 employees , and that's okay.

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:28.560
The important part of SRE for a small company is&nbsp;
not so much having someone with that job title,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:34.160
because your developers are your operators at&nbsp;
that point. It's engineering everything they do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:40.640
with that SRE mindset: that failure is an option&nbsp;
and, as a matter of fact, should be predicted for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:44.240
but is something that you can automate&nbsp;
to solve. It's something that you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:47.600
create enough redundancy that,&nbsp;
when failure does happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:51.440
it's not a big deal because you're&nbsp;
resilient enough that nothing goes down.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:58.320
And so, as long as you develop with that SRE&nbsp;
mindset in mind, and you are being resilient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:02.960
you're being redundant, you are constantly going&nbsp;
back and automating problems so that you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:08.800
have to manually fix the same thing over and over&nbsp;
and over again, and you're doing good root cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:14.480
analysis on actual failures so that they don't&nbsp;
happen again, and you're monitoring so that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:19.520
will know when they're about to happen and you can&nbsp;
head it off at the pass - that's really the key.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:24.960
Large organizations, they can afford an entire&nbsp;
SRE department. They can stand it up, or they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:30.640
can transition an existing operations group into&nbsp;
it by empowering that operations group. Again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:33.760
that 50/50 rule, spending&nbsp;
half their time automating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:39.920
half their time fixing problems, and automating&nbsp;
all of the things. Automate everything, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:45.840
the less manual work and manual intervention you&nbsp;
have the happier that SRE team is going to be.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:52.880
Thank you so much for your time today. If you&nbsp;
